# Procedure

1. Import Path from pathlib. Read the skills and contracts above. Load the problem at
   the Current problem path with `load_problem`: it returns the ProblemSpec carrying
   the definition, workload, language and platform of the problem. You should inspect definition/workload/
   language/platform carefully to guide your kernel and ABI design for evaluation. 

2. Write the implementation under submission/ from the problem's PyTorch reference.
   Build it with Kernel.from_sources using read_source_tree on submission/, the loaded
   problem, and build/. 
   Evaluate the latency of each `Kernel` using `klineage.harness.eval.evaluate`;
   Counter diagnostics from klineage.tools.profile;
   Never invent your own counters or substitute latency for them.

3. Validate against the problem's own PyTorch reference. The starting kernel has no
   sources, so measure candidates standalone with klineage.harness.evaluate; it
   checks correctness against the problem oracle and reports latency. When a
   previous candidate has been built and measured, pass it as `reference=` to get
   a paired comparison. Use evaluation and profiling results to guide further
   optimization, retaining the best validated candidate and its measurement evidence.

4. Save the best result following .klineage/message/apply.md. Return the unchanged
   input if no validated speedup was found. Ensure the delivered source bundle
   matches the measured implementation.
